          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies the sea or deep primarily with chaos, danger, death, and hostile powers, while also recognizing waters as a common symbol for turbulent peoples, multitudes, and nations, especially in prophetic and apocalyptic contexts.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book makes peoples and nations the controlling referent: below the firmament they are an unrooted, restless mass, while above it they are gathered and still, represented by the heavenly waters and sea of glass.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Sea/deep waters signify chaos and hostile disorder; in an established secondary strand, waters signify restless peoples and nations.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Sea and waters signify peoples and nations in differing conditions: restless below the firmament and gathered, still, and heavenly above it.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book extends the recognized peoples-and-nations symbolism beyond turbulent prophetic waters to Genesis's upper waters, Psalm 148's heavenly waters, and Revelation's sea of glass. It also elevates this anthropological referent over the consensus's dominant chaos/hostile-power identification and distinguishes waters by their position relative to the firmament.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction with the consensus's acknowledged nations interpretation, and the book's restless lower waters retain an association with disorder. The material difference is that chaos is no longer the central referent but a condition of unrooted peoples; heavenly, still waters are likewise identified with gathered peoples, a classification not supplied by the consensus.\",\"rationale\":\"The book is not novel because Revelation 17:15 explicitly supports, and the consensus commonly recognizes, waters as peoples and nations. It is not a match because it turns that contextual meaning into a broader system covering both lower and upper waters and the sea of glass. This preserves a recognized general referent while materially reclassifying additional cases and subordinating the consensus's primary chaos symbolism, making REFINED the best classification.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Revelation 17:15 strongly identifies one particular set of waters with peoples, but Genesis 1:6–8 and Psalm 148:4 only describe waters above the firmament, while Revelation 4:6 and 15:2 describe a sea of glass without identifying it as gathered peoples. The printed citations therefore do not by themselves establish that all these images share the same human referent or that lower peoples become the heavenly sea of glass.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
